Generic Collection
Non-generic collection

Generic collections in C# are type-safe collections that allow you to store and manipulate elements of a specific data type.
Non-generic collections in C# are collections that store elements of type object, allowing you to store objects of any type.

They are defined using generic types, which enable you to specify the type of elements that the collection can contain at compile time.
They are not type-safe, meaning that you can store any type of object in the collection without type checking at compile time.

Generic collections offer compile-time type safety and better performance by avoiding the need for boxing and unboxing operations.
Non-generic collections require explicit casting when retrieving elements, which can lead to runtime errors if the wrong type is cast.

Examples of generic collections include List<T>, Dictionary<TKey, TValue>, Queue<T>, Stack<T>, etc.
Examples of non-generic collections include ArrayList, Hashtable, Queue, Stack, etc.

